        <!-- Section 6: Conclusions -->
        <section id="conclusions">
            <h2>6. Conclusions & Limitations</h2>

            <h3>Key Findings Summary</h3>

            <ol>
                <li><strong>The exam structure has changed meaningfully.</strong> From 2015–2016 (27 questions, MCQ-heavy) to 2017–2019 (36 questions, Short-heavy) to 2021–2025 (33 questions, balanced), the board has shifted its emphasis. Educators should note the current balanced approach (45.5% MCQ / 45.5% Short / 9.1% Long) appears stable.</li>

                <li><strong>MCQ and Long counts are structurally fixed at 15 and 3 respectively</strong> — a remarkable consistency across all 10 years. Only the Short question count varies (9, 15, or 18).</li>

                <li><strong>Database and C Programming dominate the curriculum.</strong> These two domains account for the majority of question content. Within C Programming, loops, operators, functions, and control structures are the most frequently tested sub-topics.</li>

                <li><strong>Some important CS topics are under-represented:</strong> Recursion, pointers, arrays, and structures appear rarely or never — a potential gap between exam coverage and comprehensive CS education.</li>

                <li><strong>2025 shows subtle signals of new topic introduction:</strong> Bitwise operators, storage classes, binary search, and ACID properties appear in 2025 Long questions — these may indicate a curriculum expansion the board is quietly introducing.</li>
            </ol>

            <h3>Limitations</h3>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Missing Year (2020):</strong> No data is available for 2020 due to COVID-19. The gap interrupts the continuity of trend analysis.</li>
                <li><strong>No Annual vs. Supplementary Distinction:</strong> The dataset does not separate annual papers from supplementary examinations. Year-over-year comparisons may mix different paper types.</li>
                <li><strong>Keyword Frequency Limitations:</strong> Topic extraction based on keyword matching may miss questions that discuss a topic without using its exact keyword (e.g., "recursion" might appear as "recursive function").</li>
                <li><strong>No Curriculum Weighting:</strong> The dataset does not include curriculum documents specifying how much time each topic should receive. Frequency of appearance may not reflect intended instructional emphasis.</li>
                <li><strong>Community-Compiled Data:</strong> As a non-official, community-verified dataset, minor transcription errors may exist.</li>
            </ul>

            <div class="insight-box">
                <strong>Final Note:</strong> This analysis is based strictly on observed patterns in the provided dataset. No external data sources, projections, or assumptions have been introduced. All statistics and trends derive directly from the 327 questions across 10 examination years (2015–2025, excluding 2020).
            </div>
        </section>

